WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.284 --> 00:00:02.701
(classical music)

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.917
- [Narrator] You're in Mississippi

00:00:04.917 --> 00:00:07.883
and everywhere you go, tamales.

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:09.812
Those magical little snacks of corn flour

00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:12.841
and meat packed into a corn husk.

00:00:12.841 --> 00:00:15.008
Tamales, tamales, tamales.

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:19.112
So if you've ever wondered
what Mississippi tastes like,

00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:21.612
it tastes like me, the tamale.

00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:30.647
My journey starts almost
a hundred years ago.

00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.456
It's 1930 and Mexican migrants

00:00:33.456 --> 00:00:36.039
are trickling into Mississippi.

00:00:37.256 --> 00:00:39.305
They bring along a taste of home.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:43.138
Their version of the
sandwich, me, the tamale.

00:00:45.077 --> 00:00:46.678
- My great grandfather came here

00:00:46.678 --> 00:00:49.668
from Mexico City, jumped the trains,

00:00:49.668 --> 00:00:51.390
ended up in Mississippi.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.636
He decided to make his family's homemade,

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:56.231
hand rolled hot tamales and in 1939

00:00:56.231 --> 00:01:00.398
opened up this place and
started selling tamales.

00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:02.307
You have to understand
this is a family thing.

00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:04.035
I'm the fourth generation.

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:05.432
The same generation of people who

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:06.534
have been coming to the Big Apple Inn

00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:09.197
for the last 78 years
are also generational.

00:01:09.197 --> 00:01:11.123
When they eat a tamale,
they always have a story.

00:01:11.123 --> 00:01:12.485
My daddy used to bring me here.

00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:14.206
His daddy had the same kind of story,

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:17.038
his daddy had the same kind of story.

00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:21.205
Tamales caught on here and it
became a staple in the city.

00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:25.325
- [Narrator] So there I am

00:01:25.325 --> 00:01:27.219
winning over the Mississippi natives

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.572
and as I make my way up into the Delta

00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:32.035
my location may change
but little did I know

00:01:32.035 --> 00:01:36.202
that the tradition of family
most certainly would not.

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.588
Just like Geno and his
grandfather at the Big Apple Inn,

00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:41.558
I bring the Scott family together,

00:01:41.558 --> 00:01:43.838
a family who has gone on to be known

00:01:43.838 --> 00:01:47.671
as the first family of
tamales in Mississippi.

00:01:49.036 --> 00:01:50.873
- In the early '50s, my father

00:01:50.873 --> 00:01:53.948
bought the recipe from a Mexican guy.

00:01:53.948 --> 00:01:56.828
My mother and father taught me,

00:01:56.828 --> 00:01:59.778
my sisters and my niece,
all of us together,

00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:01.296
we making hot tamale.

00:02:01.296 --> 00:02:04.129
We just one big hot tamale family.

00:02:05.153 --> 00:02:07.949
Any business that's family oriented,

00:02:07.949 --> 00:02:10.116
I think it'll last longer.

00:02:12.874 --> 00:02:15.179
- [Narrator] Much like the Scott family,

00:02:15.179 --> 00:02:17.352
the Doe family is made up of a long line

00:02:17.352 --> 00:02:19.286
of tamale makers.

00:02:19.286 --> 00:02:21.496
Doe's Eat Place is a landmark restaurant

00:02:21.496 --> 00:02:23.756
that helped propel me
to become the backbone

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:27.654
of Mississippi cuisine I've become today.

00:02:27.654 --> 00:02:30.581
- My daddy started this restaurant in 1941

00:02:30.581 --> 00:02:31.813
with my mother.

00:02:31.813 --> 00:02:34.032
We've been here 76 years.

00:02:34.032 --> 00:02:37.809
Mama had a sister that was living here

00:02:37.809 --> 00:02:39.812
and daddy had a couple,
two or three sisters,

00:02:39.812 --> 00:02:41.703
that were living here so they all came

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:43.286
and rolled tamales.

00:02:44.301 --> 00:02:47.483
Yeah, a lot of people
ask us how did tamales

00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:49.907
get prevalent in the Delta.

00:02:49.907 --> 00:02:51.376
It's been family.

00:02:51.376 --> 00:02:54.376
The family's been here all the time.

00:02:55.311 --> 00:02:56.755
- [Narrator] As tamales made their way

00:02:56.755 --> 00:03:00.443
from Mexican culture to
African American communities

00:03:00.443 --> 00:03:02.986
and on to Mississippi's white population,

00:03:02.986 --> 00:03:06.285
there's one thing that
remained a constant.

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:09.702
Family, the most southern concept of all.

